# Minimal reproduction for the "stray channel error" bug: when the *last* command in a
# pipeline errors before ever sending output, Job::eval (src/lang/job.rs) used to still
# unconditionally do `context.output.send(last_input.recv()?)?` after evaluating it. By
# then the sender on the other end of that channel had been dropped, so
# `last_input.recv()` immediately returned a RecvError -- a second, unrelated
# "receiving on an empty and disconnected channel" message that leaked an implementation
# detail and drowned out (or, depending on the exact path, replaced) the real one.
# Fixed by only forwarding recv()'s value if it actually arrives.
#
# This exercises the can_block=false path (convert's handler errors synchronously,
# without spawning a thread); the can_block=true path (e.g. a failing `select` call) was
# also verified by hand to show the identical symptom, sharing the same root cause in
# job.rs, so one repro covers both.
#
# The `echo "barrier"` below is deliberate, not decorative: touching $msg with a method
# call or string concatenation in the very next statement after the catch block races
# and can itself surface the exact leaked-channel-error symptom this test guards
# against, unrelated to whether job.rs's own bug is present. Root cause not found;
# this is one of several repro shapes of the same still-open race.
$msg := ""
try {
    seq 0 3 | convert $integer "notanumber"
} catch { |$e|
    $msg = $e
}
echo "barrier"

$leaked := $($msg:like "*disconnected*")
assert ($leaked == $false) "the real error should be the only thing caught, no leaked channel error"

$len := $($msg:len)
assert ($len > 0) "expected a non-empty caught error message"
